    description: "Source: a second outside reader (a fresh LLM session given only the help output and the cached files) reviewed a hypergumbo 9.2.0 self-run on 2026-11-13, cache f36c63310c791188/results/c872eb7a7e793eab/ac34c56c06ec1788, made after the fixes from the first review merged. Each claim was re-checked against those files and the code on dev fed15705d7, then measured on 20 repositories that are not hypergumbo (default sketch and --no-source, 8k/32k/128k). Scripts, the 120 sketches and results.txt: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/outside_reader_review2_11142026.\n\nOBSERVED. The Python analyzer writes type annotations with holes. A three-line file:\n  def f(cb: Callable[[int, str], None], lit: Literal[-1, 1], ann: Annotated[int, max(1, 2)], star: tuple[*tuple[int, ...]]) -> Callable[[int], str]: ...\n  class P(namedtuple(\"P\", \"x y\")): pass\ngives\n  signature: (cb: Callable[, None], lit: Literal[, 1], ann: Annotated[int, ], star: tuple[]) -> Callable[, str]\n  meta.parameters[].type: \"Callable[, None]\", \"Literal[, 1]\", \"Annotated[int, ]\", \"tuple[]\"\n  meta.return_type: \"Callable[[int], str]\" (correct: this field is written with ast.unparse, so the two renderings of one annotation disagree)\n  P: meta.base_classes == [\"\"]\nIn sketches: hypergumbo self-run 2/4/10 occurrences of \"Callable[, \" at 8k/32k/128k; mitmproxy 3 and poetry 3 at 128k; sqlalchemy 1 at 32k and 14 at 128k. Three of the six Python repositories measured.\n\nMECHANISM. py.py _format_annotation handles Name, Constant, Subscript, Tuple, BinOp(BitOr) and Attribute, and returns \"\" for every other node, on a line marked \"pragma: no cover - defensive fallback for unknown AST types\". The fallback is reached by ordinary Python: ast.List (the parameter list of Callable), ast.UnaryOp (a negative Literal), ast.Call (Annotated metadata, a computed base class), ast.Starred. An absent rendering is written as an empty one, and the callers cannot tell. Callers: parameter types (meta.parameters, which is what consumers are told to read in place of the display signature), the signature, base classes, class attribute annotations, decorator names.\n\nCONSTRAINT. The same text is an input to identity: _format_function_signature(node) -> normalize_python_signature -> make_typed_stable_id. Correcting the text changes the stable_id of every Python function whose annotations take the fallback. By the owner's 10-01/10-23 ruling an input change to the hash is a bump. v14 is unreleased, so the owner decides between (a) correct the identity input too, inside v14 or as v15, and (b) correct display, meta.parameters and base_classes only and keep the identity input byte-stable. Count the affected functions on a corpus before that decision.\n\nASK. One renderer for an annotation (ast.unparse is total on a valid tree and is already what return_type uses), with no silent empty result: a node the renderer cannot write is either written by unparse or recorded as not rendered. Remove the no-cover pragma, since the branch is reached. Check the sibling fields the same helper feeds (base_classes with a call-expression base gives [\"\"], which a consumer reads as a base named nothing).\n\nGENERALITY (owner, 2026-11-13). The fix must be a rule that holds for any repository and any language. The hypergumbo self-run is only where the defect was first seen. A fix keyed to this repository's paths or file names, or one that works for one language only, does not close this item.\nSibling languages: the same question (does a type renderer drop the parts of a type it has no case for, and does that text feed stable_id) has to be asked of every analyzer that renders parameter types by walking the tree with an else-branch. Enumerate them; do not assume the tree-sitter analyzers are clean because they slice source text. This was not checked."  # 25af
